#host("Farid Responds", ("What is there to learn from pre-Islamic Arabia? For me personally, this is one of the reasons I'm so interested. I come across these names in the S and I had no idea about who these people were. The prophet peace be upon him in Bkari tells Ben Hatim, if you live long enough, you will live to see a woman travel from Al to the Kaa to circumambulate it and not fear anyone but Allah. Eddie said, \"So I said in my heart, but what about the raiders of that had spread across the land?\" This is a fulfilled prophecy by the way.", "But why is it significant? Why did specifically mention what did the prophet uh specifically mean when he mentioned? The answers to these questions will be provided in this series. When Khar was killed in the battle of Badr, why was Abdman b so upset? Isn't it strange that Abdman wanted this major enemy of Islam to stay alive without understanding Jah a chunk of the is hard to understand and even when it is understood we often don't understand the significance of it mentions that before the prophet peace be upon him went to hijra he approached Benu Amir with his message but it didn't work out most of us have never even heard of Benu Amar And when we just come across the name of the tribe in a page, it just means nothing to you.", "It's just a name. But when you know the history of Benu um when you come across what had mentioned, then you put your hands on your head in shock. For many people, it would simply be a matter of curiosity about this era that not so many people know much about. The most famous thing about Jahalia is that people worship multiple gods and this was something that ended when Islam emerged. For some, it could be that learning about Jahalyah played a role in their appreciation for Islam since Islam not only influenced matters to do with paganism alone, but it had a major effect on many aspects of the culture.", "Regardless of your reasons, you should be aware that Jahalia history is fascinating, especially matters that have to do with the wars between the tribes. And in this series, the major wars will be covered. For those that have studied the Sierra thoroughly, you'll even notice some names and pre-Islamic history wars making their first appearance in history. However, despite that being the case, so much of what you'll be hearing in this series, you'll be coming across for the very first time. Apart from the wars, I'll be providing you with a slice of life of pre-Islamic times in the beginning of the series in order to get a better idea of the historical context and how Arabs lived their lives.", "This will also cover um aspects of Beduin life as well as the lives of city dwellers, their businesses, their food, their customs, their beliefs, their poetry, and so on. The first episode isn't going to be too convoluted, but later on in this series, when we get to the wars, you'll be coming across a lot of names that you'll be hearing for the first time. To make things easier for you to follow, I've included an index with almost all the names that are mentioned this series with a very brief biography. Um, mainly letting you know which tribe they're from in order for you not to get lost.", "The course also um has a tribes tree in order for you to understand the relationship between these tribes. This should be useful to you. I find myself often returning to the tree uh returning to the maps that I've made in order for you to know where these tribes are located, where these cities are located with these towns where these battles are located. Uh my intention is for this course to be beneficial and efficient. And if each battle was mentioned in detail, um it would be a 100hour course. So, I'm mainly providing you information about the the most important battles and the ones that have um important lessons in order for us to learn more about Jah and pre-Islamic",))
