#host("Farid Responds", ("As-salamu alaykum, warahmatullahi wabarakatuh. In continuation of the series on the Jahiliyyah or the pre-Islamic times, I feel this is the right time to shed light on the religion and the beliefs of the people of the peninsula. I don't want to go into much detail about the religions of the northern kingdoms yet. I'm referring to the Ghassanids and Lakhmids since they were both different Christian sects, but uh I'll go into that later on, yeah. This lecture, I want to mainly focus on the beliefs and practices of the pagan Arabs themselves. As we know, the pagans worshipped idols, statues that they built with their own hands.", "But, it is important to not have a simplistic view of the pagans. Uh they didn't believe that their statues created them or anything like that. Um they believed that their idols were representatives. Ibn Abbas reports in Sahih al-Bukhari um some of the background that led to the emergence of shirk or polytheism in pre-Islamic Arabia. He said, \"All the idols which were worshipped by the people of Noah were worshipped by the Arabs later on. As for the idol Wadd, it was worshipped by the tribe of Kalb at Dumat al-Jandal. Suwa' was the idol of the tribe of Hudhayl.", "Yaghuth was the worshipped Yaghuth was worshipped by the tribe of Murad and then by Banu Ghutayf at al-Jawf near Saba. Ya'uq was the idol of Hamdan and Nasr was the idol of Himyar, the branch of Dhu al-Kila'. The names of the idols formerly belonged to some pious men of the people of Noah. And when they died, Satan inspired their people to prepare and place idols at the places where they used to sit and to call those idols by their names. The people did so, but the idols were not worshipped till those people who initiated them had died and the origin of the idols had become obscure.", "your. Whereupon people began worshipping them. If you notice what Ibn Abbas said, he mentioned that Yaghuth was first worshipped by the tribe of Murad and then it was worshipped by the tribe of Banu Ghatafan. Muhammad bin Habib al-Baghdadi, um who's an early historian, explains that Yaghuth was an idol that was worshipped by Madhhij as a whole, uh which includes multiple tribes, and it was specifically among the tribe of An'um. However, the tribe of Ghatafan uh fought them over it and then they escaped with the idol to Najran among Banu al-Harith bin Ka'b. In the reports of Tabari, Yaghuth would once be shifted over from tribe to tribe that would continuously be fighting over it.", "One of the battles ended in a lot of bloodshed and it occurred during the time of the Battle of Badr. It was known as the Day of al-Razzam. The shapes of these idols came in different forms. Wadd was in the form of a man. Suwa' was in the form of a woman and al-Lat was a white cube with carvings. By the way, these were the major idols. Um there were smaller idols that people kept in their homes as well and apparently it was uh quite common. Pagans from around the peninsula would even purchase idols from the people of Mecca.", "In another report, Ibn Hisham narrates from the people of knowledge of his time that Amr bin Luhai uh left Mecca towards Sham and when he was in al-Balqa, which is modern-day Jordan, he met some Amalekites um that were worshipping idols and he asked them, \"What are these idols that you're worshipping?\" So, they said, \"These are idols that we worship. If we ask for rain, then rain comes down for us. And if we ask for victory, then victory comes to us.\" So, he said, \"Do you mind giving me an idol to take to the land of the Arabs uh for them to worship?\"", "So, they gave him an idol named Hubal. So, he brought it to Mecca and told the people to worship and glorify it. Amr bin Luhai is the father of Khuza'a, which means that he passed away 300 years before Islam. This means that Hubal was brought to Mecca relatively late. For a comprehensive list of the idols, um there are two recommended works that I would suggest. The first is Kitab al-Asnam by Ibn al-Kalbi and uh even though he's not generally a reliable historian, um but there's little reason for us to assume that the information contained within are fabrications.", "Um and the second is a contemporary work called Mu'jam Alihat al-Arab Qabl al-Islam, um which is a modern work and it gets information from various sources.",))
#source("Source: Farid Responds, \"The Religion and Superstitions of pre-Islamic Arabs\"")
